   "gap": "Supply Grinstead and Snell's stated equivalent form of mutual independence (Def. 4.2, p. 141: A1,...,An are mutually independent exactly when, for every choice of each Ai or its complement, the probability of the full intersection is the product of the probabilities; the case n = 3 is Exercise 33, p. 155) and their claim that the single identity P(A1 and ... and An) = P(A1)...P(An) does not imply mutual independence (p. 141, with the six-point example of Exercise 8, p. 151). The definition entry gives only the subset-product form; on the route the general chapter proves only that complementing independent events preserves independence (thm:p15-independence-basic, item 2) and remarks that checking the full intersection alone is not the definition (def:p15-general-independence), so neither the converse equivalence nor the non-implication with a counterexample is supplied.",
